The 500 mm height is a starting design value, not a validated optimum.

You need to calculate the camera footprint from its actual lens field of view.

For a downward camera:

\\[ W=2H\tan\left(\frac{FOV_x}{2}\right) \\]

\\[ L=2H\tan\left(\frac{FOV_y}{2}\right) \\]

This tells you how much ground the camera sees at its mounting height.

**One important correction:** Your existing calibration maps pixels to gantry coordinates. That transformation cannot simply be reused for a moving rover without accounting for rover pose and camera extrinsics.

Also, `servo_gimbal.py` currently uses proportional pixel-to-servo mapping. It does not implement actual geometric camera-to-treatment-head calibration.

The state machine calculates calibrated X/Y coordinates but doesn't use them to control the treatment mechanism.

That means the calculated coordinates and physical aiming are currently disconnected.

