The software should model this as a treatment module, not as a laser.

Suggested interface:

```
class FlameTreatment:    def prepare(self, target):        pass    def activate(self):        pass    def deactivate(self):        pass    def emergency_disable(self):        pass    def get_status(self):        pass
```

I would separate the rotation controller from the activation controller.

Conceptually:

- Rotation controller: controls the treatment head's permitted position.
- Activation controller: requests treatment only when all safety conditions are satisfied.
- Safety controller: independently prevents activation when conditions aren't satisfied.

**Critical safety consideration:** An open-flame treatment system operating around dry vegetation, crop leaves, people and fuel creates a substantial fire hazard. A software stop button alone is not sufficient.

Before physical implementation, this needs an independently engineered flame shutoff, hardware interlock, fail-closed control, fire detection, suitable suppression provisions, guarded exclusion zone and supervised testing. The robot must not activate the flame system when its target or surroundings are uncertain.

I would not reuse the existing laser controller as the flame controller.

